```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a crashed (or externally killed) bgpd process on leaf_router_0_0.
The /etc/frr/daemons file explicitly sets bgpd=yes, so the daemon is supposed to
be running, but a process-list inspection shows only zebra, mgmtd, and staticd
alive — bgpd is completely absent. Without bgpd, leaf_router_0_0 cannot establish
BGP sessions with either spine router. Both spine_router_0_0 and spine_router_0_1
confirm this: their neighbor entries for leaf_router_0_0's addresses are stuck in
"Active" state with 0 prefixes received and 0 prefixes sent, while the sessions
toward leaf_router_0_1 and super_spine_router_0 are fully Established.

The consequence propagates through the entire fabric: leaf_router_0_0 never
advertises its directly connected 10.0.0.0/24 prefix into BGP, so neither spine
nor leaf_router_0_1 installs a route to that subnet. Symmetrically,
leaf_router_0_0's own RIB contains only its three connected prefixes and has no
path to 10.0.1.0/24. When pc_0_0 sends traffic toward pc_0_1, leaf_router_0_0
immediately returns "Destination Net Unreachable" — exactly what the helpdesk
ticket describes.

All physical interfaces (eth0, eth1, eth2) on leaf_router_0_0 are UP/LOWER_UP,
which disproves the team's cable-fault hypothesis; no physical reseating is needed.

Root cause: the bgpd daemon process on leaf_router_0_0 is not running, leaving the
device with no BGP participation in the fabric.

Corrective fix: restart the FRR service on leaf_router_0_0
(e.g., `service frr restart` or `systemctl restart frr`). This will re-launch
bgpd as specified in /etc/frr/daemons, re-establish the BGP sessions with both
spines, and restore full prefix exchange so that pc_0_0 ↔ pc_0_1 traffic flows
again.
=== END ===
```